[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The adviser is built for tasks that are mostly easy with a few genuinely hard corners. Here is the insight most people are going to miss. With the adviser,'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'timing is everything. The advice is most valuable at two very specific moments. The first is early, right after the model has looked around and gathered a'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little context, but before it commits to an approach. That first call is where the plan gets set. The second is at the very end, right before the model declares the job finished as a final'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'review of the work it just did. Calling too early before the model has even read the problem wastess the advice on an empty transcript. Calling on every trivial step just burns money. For most'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hard tasks, the sweet spot is two to three calls, one to plan and one to check. Now, the models do not all behave the same way here. And this is genuinely worth knowing. The fast haiku model'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tends to be shy. Left on its own, it often forgets to ask for help on exactly the coding tasks where help would have rescued it. So Antropic found a simple fix. After Haiku\'s first move, if it'

[08:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'still has not consulted the adviser, you slip in a gentle reminder, one short sentence that says, "You have not asked the adviser yet. If there is a real'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decision here, ask now." In their testing, that one nudge lifted Haiku\'s success rate by around seven points. But here is the twist. That very same nudge does nothing for Sonnet. And it actually'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'makes OPUS slightly worse because OPUS already knows when to ask. So nudge the junior and trust the senior. Let me save you some money now because this is where'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people get surprised by their bill. The adviser is built separately at the adviser models rate for the little bursts of thinking it does and by default that thinking can run long. So,'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there is one setting you should almost always turn on. You cap the advisor\'s output with a max tokens of around 2,000. In Antropic\'s own testing, that'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single cap cut the advisor\'s output by roughly seven times with almost no loss in quality. You can also write inside your message. Simply ask the adviser to'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keep it under 80 words, a focused starting point, not an essay. And if your task runs long and calls the adviser three or more times, switch on'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'caching so it stops repaying to reread the same history. Cap it, ask for brevity, cash the long ones, and the adviser stays cheap. Step back for a second and look at what this really is.'

[10:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For years, the trade-off was carved in stone. You paid for intelligence by the token on every single token. The advisor tool breaks that. It lets you spend genius intelligence surgically only in'

